{"causes": ["The conversion of Visigothic King Reccared I from Arianism to Nicene Christianity in 587 CE necessitated new orthodox episcopal appointments across Hispania.", "The strategic importance of Zaragoza as a major urban and ecclesiastical center in the Ebro Valley required a strong and learned bishop to consolidate Visigothic authority.", "The intellectual and theological ferment of late antique Hispania fostered an environment where scholars like Maximus could emerge as influential chroniclers and church leaders."], "effects": ["Maximus's episcopate helped consolidate Nicene Christianity and royal authority in the strategically vital region of Zaragoza within the Visigothic Kingdom.", "His *Chronicon Caesaraugustanum* provided an essential primary source for later Hispano-Visigothic historians, preserving a unique historical record of the era.", "Maximus contributed to the intellectual and theological development of the Visigothic Church, shaping its identity during a period of significant transformation."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "reccared-i", "sourceName": "Reccared I", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "maximus", "targetName": "Maximus", "context": "King Reccared I's conversion to Nicene Christianity and his ecclesiastical policies directly shaped the environment in which Maximus rose to prominence as bishop of Zaragoza."}, {"sourceSlug": "maximus", "sourceName": "Maximus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "zaragoza", "targetName": "Zaragoza", "context": "Maximus served as the first Visigothic bishop of Zaragoza from 592 to 619 CE, making the city the primary locus of his ecclesiastical and intellectual activities."}, {"sourceSlug": "maximus", "sourceName": "Maximus", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "chronicon-caesaraugustanum", "targetName": "Chronicon Caesaraugustanum", "context": "Maximus is widely attributed as the author of the *Chronicon Caesaraugustanum*, a significant historical chronicle covering the years 450 to 613 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "isidore-of-seville", "sourceName": "Isidore of Seville", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "maximus", "targetName": "Maximus", "context": "Isidore of Seville, a contemporary and later intellectual giant, likely influenced Maximus through the broader Hispano-Visigothic intellectual tradition, though Maximus predates Isidore's major works."}, {"sourceSlug": "maximus", "sourceName": "Maximus", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "classical-learning", "targetName": "Classical Learning", "context": "Through his historical writings and theological work, Maximus helped transmit elements of classical learning and historical methodology into the early medieval Visigothic context."}], "places": [{"name": "Zaragoza, Spain", "role": "Episcopal See"}, {"name": "Toledo, Spain", "role": "Visigothic Capital"}, {"name": "Hispania", "role": "Geographic Region"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Maximus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "maximus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3109132", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximus_(bishop_of_Zaragoza)"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Maximus's role as the first Visigothic bishop of Zaragoza and author of the *Chronicon Caesaraugustanum* was regionally significant, providing a crucial historical account and contributing to the consolidation of Nicene Christianity in Hispania.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-16T01:43:36Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29464611373", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Maximus was the first Visigothic bishop of Zaragoza (Hispania) in 592–619. He was also a theologian and historian.", "newValue": "Maximus, the inaugural Visigothic bishop of Zaragoza from 592 until his death in 619 CE, stands as a pivotal intellectual figure in early medieval Hispania. As an ecclesiastical leader, theologian, and historian, he contributed significantly to the consolidation of the Nicene Visigothic Church and the intellectual life of the kingdom after the conversion of King Reccared I. His tenure coincided with a period of intense religious and political transformation, where the Visigothic state sought to integrate its Hispano-Roman populace under a unified, orthodox Christian identity.

During his episcopate, Maximus actively participated in the synods and councils that shaped the Visigothic Church, notably the Second Council of Zaragoza (592 CE), which addressed issues of ecclesiastical discipline and the establishment of Nicene orthodoxy. He is widely credited with authoring the *Chronicon Caesaraugustanum* (Chronicle of Zaragoza), a vital historical work covering events from 450 to 613 CE. This chronicle, though brief, provides invaluable insights into the political machinations, ecclesiastical appointments, and natural phenomena of the era, offering a unique Visigothic perspective on late antique and early medieval history.

